@startuml
' Figure - Activity Diagram: the lifecycle transition validation pipeline
!include _header.txt

start
:Receive (current state, target state,
requesting account, justification);

if (pair present in the transition table?) then (no)
  :Reject: "no such transition";
  :Record the rejection in the audit trail;
  stop
else (yes)
endif

:Look up the entry:
required functions, preconditions;

if (requesting account's function
is among the required ones?) then (no)
  :Reject naming the function required;
  :Record the rejection in the audit trail;
  stop
else (yes)
endif

while (another precondition to evaluate?) is (yes)
  :Evaluate the precondition;
  if (satisfied?) then (no)
    :Reject naming the EXACT condition unmet;
    :Record the rejection in the audit trail;
    stop
  else (yes)
  endif
endwhile (no)

if (justification supplied?) then (no)
  :Reject: justification required;
  stop
else (yes)
endif

:Apply the new state;
:Write the custody and audit records;
:Re-run the risk assessment so the new
state is reflected immediately;
:Commit;
:Return success;
stop

note right
  A rejection is written to the audit trail as
  readily as a success. An attempt that failed
  is forensically interesting in its own right
  (SR-29).
end note
@enduml
